   "source": [
    "pack_segment1 = segment1.groupby('PACK_SIZE')['PROD_QTY'].sum() / qty_segment1\n",
    "pack_other = other.groupby('PACK_SIZE')['PROD_QTY'].sum() / qty_other\n",
    "\n",
    "pack_affinity = pd.DataFrame({'target_segment': pack_segment1, 'other': pack_other})\n",
    "pack_affinity['affinity'] = pack_affinity['target_segment'] / pack_affinity['other']\n",
    "pack_affinity = pack_affinity.sort_values('affinity', ascending=False)\n",
    "pack_affinity"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "id": "69327282",
   "source": [
    "Statistically test whether Mainstream singles/couples pay a genuinely different average price per unit than other customers, using a t-test (which tells us if the price difference is likely a real pattern or just random variation)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 77,
   "id": "fabb631f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "t-statistic: 39.92\n",
      "p-value: 0.0\n",
      "Mean price - Mainstream: 3.9997\n",
      "Mean price - Other: 3.6442\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from scipy import stats\n",
    "\n",
    "group_mainstream = merged[\n",
    "    (merged['LIFESTAGE'].isin(['YOUNG SINGLES/COUPLES', 'MIDAGE SINGLES/COUPLES'])) &\n",
    "    (merged['PREMIUM_CUSTOMER'] == 'Mainstream')\n",
    "]['PRICE_PER_UNIT']\n",
    "\n",
    "group_other = merged[\n",
    "    (merged['LIFESTAGE'].isin(['YOUNG SINGLES/COUPLES', 'MIDAGE SINGLES/COUPLES'])) &\n",
    "    (merged['PREMIUM_CUSTOMER'] != 'Mainstream')\n",
    "]['PRICE_PER_UNIT']\n",
    "\n",
    "t_stat, p_value = stats.ttest_ind(group_mainstream, group_other, equal_var=False)\n",
    "print(f\"t-statistic: {t_stat:.2f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"p-value: {p_value}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Mean price - Mainstream: {group_mainstream.mean():.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Mean price - Other: {group_other.mean():.4f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d7843dc5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Summary of Initial Findings\n",
    "\n",
    "**Data cleaning:**\n",
    "- Removed one non-chip product (\"Old El Paso Salsa Dip,\" ~9,324 transactions) while preserving legitimate salsa-flavored chip products.\n",
    "- Removed one outlier customer (loyalty card 226000) who purchased 200 units of the same product on two separate occasions \u2014 consistent with commercial/bulk buying rather than typical retail behaviour.\n",
    "- Standardized inconsistent brand names (e.g. RRD/RED \u2192 Red Rock Deli, WW \u2192 Woolworths, SMITH \u2192 Smiths) to avoid fragmenting brand-level analysis.\n",
    "- Final cleaned dataset: 255,510 transactions across 8 product brands... (adjust to your actual final counts)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key findings:**\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Older Families \u2013 Budget is the top-selling segment overall** ($160,795 in total sales), despite not having the highest customer count (4,641 customers, fewer than several other segments). This segment spends nearly double per customer compared to Young Singles/Couples \u2013 Mainstream, indicating the segment's strength comes from per-customer value, not sheer size.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Young Singles/Couples \u2013 Mainstream and Retirees \u2013 Mainstream drive their high total sales through a different mechanism: customer count.** These two segments have the largest customer bases (7,955 and 6,421 customers respectively) \u2014 their high total sales reflect volume of shoppers, not unusually high individual spend.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Household size, not premium tier, is the primary driver of purchase volume.** Family segments (Older Families, Young Families) buy roughly 9 units per customer on average \u2014 nearly double the ~4.3\u20134.7 units bought by Singles/Couples segments \u2014 regardless of Budget/Mainstream/Premium classification.\n",
    "\n",
    "4. **Price per unit and pack size are remarkably consistent across segments overall** (price: $3.59\u2013$4.03; pack size: 176.6g\u2013179.8g), suggesting most segments gravitate toward similar product choices.\n",
    "\n",
    "5. **However, within Young/Midage Singles-Couples specifically, Mainstream customers pay a statistically significant premium** \u2014 $4.00/unit vs. $3.64/unit for their Budget/Premium counterparts (independent t-test: t=39.92, p\u22480). This indicates a genuine willingness among Mainstream shoppers in this age group to pay more, likely reflecting impulse or brand-driven purchasing rather than value-seeking behaviour.\n",
    "\n",
    "6. **Kettle is the top-selling brand across every single customer segment**, indicating broad, universal brand appeal rather than segment-specific loyalty.\n",
    "\n",
    "7. **Young Singles/Couples \u2013 Mainstream customers show distinct brand preferences relative to the rest of the customer base:** they are ~24.5% more likely to buy Tyrrells and ~23.6% more likely to buy Twisties, and ~55% less likely to buy Burger Rings. Their apparent preference for 270g packs is explained by this same Twisties affinity, as Twisties is the only brand sold in that pack size \u2014 a reminder that pack-size patterns can be proxies for brand preference rather than independent size preference.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Commercial implications for Julia:**\n",
    "- **Older Families \u2013 Budget** represents the highest per-customer value and should be prioritized with multi-buy or loyalty-based promotions to capitalize on their already-high purchase frequency.\n",
    "- **Young Singles/Couples \u2013 Mainstream and Retirees \u2013 Mainstream** are high-value due to customer volume \u2014 growth strategies here should focus on customer acquisition and retention rather than increasing spend per visit.\n",
    "- The Category Manager could consider **off-locating Tyrrells and Twisties** in discretionary/impulse spaces frequented by young singles and couples, leveraging their demonstrated brand affinity and statistically higher willingness to pay.\n",
    "- Since pack size preference doesn't vary meaningfully by segment (once brand effects are accounted for), pack-size-based marketing (e.g. \"family size\" vs. \"single serve\") is likely less impactful than frequency-, volume-, or brand-based incentives.\n",
    "- Kettle's universal popularity supports continued strong shelf presence/stock priority regardless of store demographic mix.\n"
   ]
  }
 ],
